# Deployment rules — read before spawning any agent

One copy. Every `TASK.md` references this file rather than restating it; if a task's rules and
this file disagree, **this file wins and one of them needs fixing.**

## 0 · Precedence

1. `<operating invariants file>` — how work is run
2. **The running system** — live code, config, data. Beats every document.
3. `<durable project memory>` — closed decisions
4. This plan
5. `<handoff / spec documents>` — written against a snapshot; assume drift

Say out loud when two sources disagree.

## 1 · What every agent gets

**Every agent receives a directory, not a prompt.** Its task folder — `tasks/<ID>/` — is the
briefing. `TASK.md` is the contract; every artefact the agent produces is written back into the
same folder. Inbox and outbox are one place.

This holds no matter how many tasks there are. **One folder per task, always** — and a task
created mid-wave gets `mkdir tasks/<ID>/` **before its first artefact**, which is the point at
which this rule is actually broken in practice. The layout is
fixed because three separate mechanisms depend on it: containment (§2a) scopes writes to it,
the output contract (§4) files into it, and the state reporter globs `tasks/*/TASK.md` for status.
A task file living loose, or two tasks sharing a folder, breaks all three at once.

```
tasks/<ID>/TASK.md · CLAUDE.md · FINDINGS.md · CHANGES.md · QUESTIONS.md · <fragments>
```

`TASK.md` is the **contract** — what to do. `CLAUDE.md` is the **calibration** — how to behave
while doing it, and the only one of the two a harness loads by itself (§1b).

Every agent also inherits `<inventory folder>` — the frozen map of what already exists. **No agent
re-derives it.** If an agent starts searching for something already in there, the prompt failed.

The contract in `TASK.md` carries: a **persona** · the **exact files it owns** · the **evidence
already gathered** (cited) · what **"done"** means in checkable evidence · an explicit
**do-NOT list** · its **output contract**. **If a fact is needed and is neither in the folder nor
cited from the inventory, the briefing failed** — there is no ambient context to fall back on.

## 1a · Skills are starting context — invoke them FIRST

| Skill | Carries |
|---|---|
| `<skill>` | `<the scar tissue it holds>` |

An agent that reads its skills after it starts has already made the decisions the skill existed
to inform.

## 1b · The calibration file, and the caps behind it

`tasks/<ID>/CLAUDE.md` carries four behaviours that a task contract cannot state because they are
not about the task: **response length**, **narration cadence**, **scope**, and **delegation**.
They are in a file named `CLAUDE.md` for one mechanical reason — a harness auto-loads that name
from its working directory and auto-loads nothing else. Every other file in the folder has to be
read on request, and a rule that has to be requested is a preference.

<!-- WHY THESE FOUR AND NOT OTHERS. They are the four things that stopped being free in the
     Claude 5 generation. Effort no longer controls visible verbosity, so length has to be asked
     for; the model narrates readily; it expands scope on its own initiative; and it reaches for
     subagents on work that does not need them. Earlier generations needed none of these lines
     and this file correctly did not carry them. -->

**Do not restate this file inside it.** The calibration file says how to behave; these rules say
how the work is run. A second copy of §2a or §3 in ninety task folders is ninety files that will
disagree with this one, and the disagreement will be discovered by a worker who followed the
wrong one.

**The delegation cap is not in that file, and that is the point.** The runner sets it in the
dispatch environment from its runtimes row — on Claude Code, `CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_CONCURRENT_SUBAGENTS`,
`CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_SUBAGENT_SPAWN_DEPTH`, `CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_SUBAGENTS_PER_SESSION` —
and it pins every subagent to the task's own model: `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL`, filled per
dispatch from the persona file's `model:` or the task's **Model:**, with
`CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL_FORCE` on so a worker cannot ask for another. (FORCE is a switch,
not a model; set to a model name on its own it pins nothing, and this template said otherwise
for four days.) Prose says *whether* delegating is a good idea and can be
ignored; the environment says *how many* and cannot. Principle 11's rule applies to this file as
much as to a plan: a rule a machine cannot check is a preference, so the half that can be checked
is checked, and only the judgment stays in prose.

## 1c · Every agent that finishes is debriefed — by a model that did not do the work

When a subagent or a fan-out agent finishes, and when a dispatched task's own worker does, a
hook hands its transcript to `claude-haiku-4-5`, which writes four sections into this task's
`debriefs/` folder: **Key points of success** · **Unfinished business, marked defects or
failures** · **Important notes** · **Full chat workflow flaws**.

Two properties make it worth having, and both come from who writes it. The summariser did not
do the work, so it has no stake in the work looking finished; and it is the same cheap model
every time, so a hundred debriefs can be compared with each other, which a hundred self-reports
never can. **It is a note, not a verdict** — SUSPECTED until somebody checks it against the
transcript, and nothing grades a task on it. Installed from Smokin:
`templates/debrief-on-subagent-stop.sh`.

## 2 · Model tiers

**The pairing per persona lives in [`_ROSTER.md`](_ROSTER.md), with the reason.** Tier words
are not models — an orchestrator cannot dispatch `mid`. Name the identifier.

- **Recon, inventory, sweeps, drift checks** — `claude-sonnet-5` at `high`. Not lower: the
  worst misses in this method's history were on work priced as routine.
- **Bounded implementation, decisions already made** — `claude-sonnet-5` at `high`.
- **Architecture, integration, instruments, and every adversarial pass** — `claude-opus-5` at
  `xhigh`; `max` for anything that moves money, grants access, or cannot be undone.

**Effort is never below `high`.** If that feels wasteful, the task is probably a shell command
wearing a persona.

**Two adversarial passes on anything that moves money or grants access**, the second with fresh
context and no sight of the first. One independent pass is a filter, not a proof.

**Declare the tier out loud** when you fan out — publish an agent→model table. The requester
cannot see a subagent's tier and must not have to ask.

**Onward delegation is capped in the environment, not stated in prose** — §1b, and the runner's
`runtimes.json` `env` block. This line used to say to write *"do NOT spawn sub-agents"* into each
task file, which is where that sentence spent a year doing nothing: it named no mechanism, and
an agent that spawns its own sub-agents picks their models itself unless something stops it.
Something now does. Set the caps once, at the runtime, and let the calibration file carry the
judgment about *whether* a fan-out is worth it at all.

Two things still belong here rather than in a cap. Say which tier a sub-agent should run at when
this plan wants one different from the runtime's forced default — the cap pins a model, it cannot
know that this plan's fan-outs are adversarial reads that need the higher tier. And say when
delegation is forbidden for a reason a number cannot express: read-only, one per source file,
never per finding.

## 2b · Substrate

**Declare which substrate this run uses, and stick to it.** Mixing them mid-run is how a fleet
becomes half-visible.

- **Programmatic fan-out** (default) — a script spawns and collects. Cheap and deterministic;
  blind in the middle.
- **Managed terminal session** (secondary) — named agents in panes with queryable lifecycle.
  See [`_HERDR.md`](_HERDR.md). Costs a pane per agent; makes `blocked` observable and lets the
  fleet outlive the orchestrator's context.

Phase 8 says measure rather than assume. If this project has not measured both yet, say so here
rather than presenting the default as a decision.

**Whichever is used, every agent is addressable by a role name.** An unnamed fleet is a
coincidence, not an organisation.

## 2a · Containment

- Agents work in an isolated copy, **never in the deployed checkout**.
- **Read anywhere except what your own task's `Must not read:` field names; write only in your
  worktree and your task folder.** That field *narrows* this default — it does not revoke it, and it
  is not a containment boundary, so a lookup outside it is a scope question, not a violation. A
  worker that read the two as the same thing reported a legitimate lookup as a breach.
- A defect in a file you do not own gets **recorded, not fixed**.
- **Contended files have one owner and it is never a task agent.** Others emit fragments; the
  integrator applies them.

## 3 · The universal do-NOT list — verbatim in every prompt

- Do NOT deploy, restart services, run a gate, run a build, or promote a build. The
  orchestrator does all of it.
- Do NOT touch production data. Use a disposable copy.
- Do NOT certify your own work.
- Do NOT print, quote or reproduce any secret. Report only set/empty.
- Do NOT <project-specific irreversible action>.
- Do NOT <project-specific wrong-source-of-truth trap>.

## 3a · Constraints & the trap ledger — two lists, kept apart

**Project constraints — written into the plan.** The don't-touch list, compliance hard-stops,
approval gates. These live in the plan itself, next to the work they bound — not folded into
the do-NOT list above, which stays universal and verbatim.

**The standing trap ledger — pointed at, never retyped.** A running file *per domain*,
referenced by path from every plan in that domain, not copied into any of them. Domain-labelled,
because traps do not cross domains: a shell ledger — inline interpreter calls blocked by a hook
(use a script file instead), a batch find/replace that silently half-applies (`sed` without
`/g`), a zero-match count that kills a command chain (`grep -c`) — is pure noise to a content
migration, whose real traps are organisational.

A trap worth hard-coding into every prompt stays in §3, above, verbatim. The ledger is for the
rest — the ones nobody thought to write down until the second time.

Starting a new domain? Start its ledger with the first trap you hit, and label it.

This project's ledger: `<path to the domain trap ledger>`

## 4 · Output contract

All three are written **into the agent's own task folder** — `tasks/<ID>/` — never elsewhere.

| File | When | Contents |
|---|---|---|
| `FINDINGS.md` | always | cite `file:line`; **CONFIRMED** vs **SUSPECTED**; ends with "what I did not test" |
| `CHANGES.md` | if code changed | one row per file: **Did / Why / Risk**, three lines, not a diff |
| `QUESTIONS.md` | if blocked **or diverged** | one question per heading, each with a recommendation |

Task **status** is not an output file — it is a line inside each `TASK.md`, updated in place. See
§8 and §9.

## 5 · A task that can converge carries a loop — by agreement, not by enforcement

<!-- THIS HEADING USED TO READ "Every task has a loop", AND THAT WAS NOT TRUE OF ANYTHING.
     No check reads `## Loop`; OPERATING-THE-PLAN.md §11 registers the rule as ADVISORY, and
     narrower than this section claimed — its row is "Repairs go through the converge loop",
     not "every task has one". The shipped examples split the same way: all eight tasks of
     examples/a-real-first-plan carry `## Loop`, the one-task and research-first examples do
